.TH USER 9
.SH NAME
user \- the door: syscall entry, processes, the loader, notes, segments
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B import "kernel:user"
.PP
.nf
.B "dispatch :: proc(frame: ^arch.Trap_Frame)"
.B "copy_in  :: proc(addr: uintptr, n: int, dst: []u8) -> bool"
.B "copy_out :: proc(addr: uintptr, src: []u8) -> bool"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "Process :: struct { space: ^mem.Address_Space, ns: ^vfs.Namespace,"
.B "    fdt: ^Fd_Table, env: ^env.Group, pid: u64, parent: u64,"
.B "    segs: [MAX_PROC_SEGS]^Segment, exit: Exit, ... }"
.B "MAX_PROCESSES :: 256"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "load_program :: proc(p: ^Process, ns: ^vfs.Namespace, path: string) ->"
.B "    (entry: uintptr, sp: uintptr, arg0: u64, err: vectra9.Errno)"
.B "load_held :: proc(name: string, code: []u8) -> (^Process, mem.Error)"
.B "launch    :: proc(p: ^Process, arg: u64 = 0, arg2: u64 = 0) -> bool"
.B "load      :: proc(name: string, code: []u8, arg: u64 = 0, arg2: u64 = 0) ->"
.B "    (^Process, mem.Error)"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "spawn_path :: proc(parent: ^Process, path: string, flags: u64 = 0,"
.B "    argv: ^Argv = nil) -> (^Process, vfs.Errno)"
.B "sys_rfork  :: proc(frame: ^arch.Trap_Frame, flags: u64) -> i64"
.B "sys_exec   :: proc(frame: ^arch.Trap_Frame, addr: uintptr, length: int,"
.B "    argv_addr: uintptr, argc: int) -> i64"
.fi
.PP
.nf
.B "post_note     :: proc(p: ^Process, text: string) -> bool"
.B "wait_pid      :: proc(caller_pid: u64, pid: u64, patience: int) -> i64"
.B "reap_orphans  :: proc() -> (collected: int)"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.I User
is the door between ring 3 and the kernel, and everything on the kernel's
side of it: the process record, the loader, notes, and the segments a
process's memory is made of.
.SS The door and the calling convention
A program enters through
.B syscall
and the stub it lands on, described in
.IR arch (9).
The call number is
.BR rax ,
the first three arguments are
.BR rdi ", " rsi ", " rdx ,
and the next three are
.BR r10 ", " r8 ", " r9 .
.B r10
stands in for
.B rcx
because
.B syscall
puts the return address there before the stub can save it.
The answer comes back in
.BR rax :
zero or more is a result, and a negative number is
.RI \- errno
out of
.IR vectra9 (2).
Only
.BR rax ", " rcx
and
.B r11
change.
Every other register is held across the call in a
.B Trap_Frame
the stub builds.
.I Dispatch
is where the stub lands, in ordinary thread context with interrupts back
on.
It checks for a pending note before it reads
.B rax
at all: a note flagged on the thread aborts the call about to run rather
than letting it start, and the program's answer is
.BR EINTR .
.PP
Adding a system call touches three places: a number in
.B sys/abi/abi.odin
.RI ( "SYS_" NAME ),
a case in
.IR dispatch 's
switch that calls a
.BI sys_ name
handler, and a wrapper in
.IR sys/libuser
that a ring 3 program calls by name.
The three-argument shape a handler takes is whatever its call needs, not a
fixed signature: a handler reads its own arguments out of the six the
convention hands it.
.SS copy_in, copy_out and the User bit
A pointer a program hands the kernel is a number until it is checked.
.I Copy_in
and
.I copy_out
are the only ways a handler reads or writes through one, and both defer to
.IR reachable ,
which asks one question in both directions: is every page in the range
inside the half of the address space a program may name, present, and
marked
.B User
in the page tables.
A page not marked
.B User
is refused exactly as ring 3's own hardware would refuse it, which is what
makes the check equivalent to letting the program try it itself.
.I Copy_out
resolves a copy-on-write page before it writes, since a write is what a
shared page's read-only mapping exists to catch.
Every handler that touches program memory goes through one of these two
calls.
There is no bypass.
.SS The process record
A
.B Process
holds an address space, a namespace, a descriptor group, an environment
group, an exit record, note state, and up to
.B MAX_PROC_SEGS
.RI ( "forty" )
segments.
The table is
.B processes
.RI [ MAX_PROCESSES ] ,
fixed at
.B 256
records, and a live one is claimed from it by
.IR claim_slot .
.B pid
comes from
.BR next_pid ,
a counter that only ever increases, so a slot's reuse never hands out a
pid a live record or a
.I wait
still names.
.B parent
is zero for a process the kernel started directly, which is what keeps
.I wait
from letting ring 3 collect one.
.B detached
marks a process the kernel reaps rather than a parent: true for a child
forked with
.BR RFNOWAIT ,
and for one reparented when its own parent went.
.PP
.B Exit
records how a process ended: a fault's
.BR kind ", " vector " and " error_code ,
or a deliberate
.B status
from
.BR SYS_EXIT ,
or the text a program handed
.BR SYS_EXITS ,
or that a note ended it.
.B done
is set once, and is what
.I wait_pid
and
.I reap_orphans
both poll for.
.SS The loader
.I Load_program
reads a file through a namespace and builds a process's whole memory
image from it, in one of two formats.
.B VECTRA01
is a single page of position-bound code: a magic, an entry address, and a
text length, used by the blobs in
.BR kernel/user/programs .
.B VECTRA02
is a segment table, one to four rows of a virtual address, a file size, a
memory size and permission flags, used by a program a compiler built.
Every frame the loader allocates is attached to the process record before
anything can fail, so a partial load is exactly what
.I unload
already knows how to give back.
.PP
.I Load_held
does everything short of starting a thread: a new address space, a
namespace forked from the boot namespace, a descriptor group, an
environment group, and the three one-page segments a
.B VECTRA01
blob needs, with the code copied into the text page.
.I Launch
puts a thread on a held program, split out from
.I load_held
because staging bytes into a program's memory after its thread exists
races the program for that memory: nothing may touch the pages once a
thread can be scheduled.
.I Load
is
.I load_held
then
.IR launch ,
for a caller that has nothing to stage in between.
.SS spawn, rfork and exec
.I Spawn_path
starts a process from a file named in the caller's own namespace: a new
process with fresh text, data and stack, inheriting the namespace
(shared, copied, or clean, by flag), a copy of the caller's descriptor
group, and a fresh environment group.
This is Plan 9's
.I rfork
and
.I exec
fused into one call.
.PP
.I Sys_rfork
is the creating half taken back out: a second process returns from the
same call, at the same instruction, with the trap frame, the FXSAVE image
and the kernel stack all copied so the child's stack addresses mean what
the parent's did.
.B RFPROC
makes the child.
Without it the flags act on the caller.
.B RFMEM
shares writable data segments instead of copying them.
A stack is never
shared regardless of any flag, because two threads resuming through one
stack would unwind each other.
.BR RFFDG / RFCFDG ,
.BR RFNAMEG / RFCNAMEG
and
.BR RFENVG / RFCENVG
copy or clear the descriptor group, the namespace and the environment
group respectively.
With none of a pair, the child shares its parent's.
.B RFNOTEG
puts the child in a fresh note group of its own.
.B RFNOWAIT
detaches the child at birth: its
.B parent
is zero and
.B detached
is set, so no
.I wait
will ever collect it and
.I reap_orphans
does instead.
.PP
.I Sys_exec
replaces the calling process's program in place.
The pid, the descriptors and the namespace survive.
Text, data and stack
do not, and a registered note handler is cleared, since it pointed into
text that no longer exists.
The new image is built in a fresh address space before anything of the
old one is touched, so a bad file or a machine out of memory leaves the
caller running, unchanged, with an error.
Past the point where the new image is committed nothing may fail.
.SS Notes
A note is delivered at one of two boundaries: the door, which aborts the
call about to run and answers
.B EINTR
to the frame beneath it, or the tick, which redirects the interrupted
frame directly, in interrupt context.
.I Post_note
flags a note on a process's thread and returns without waiting for
delivery.
A caller that wants the process gone waits on its exit
separately.
.I Post_note
refuses a process that has already ended, since the note would
outlive its target.
.PP
A process with no registered handler ends at whichever boundary catches
the note, exactly as before notes existed.
One that called
.B notify
gets
.IR deliver_note :
the note's text and a copy of the trap frame go onto the program's own
stack, and the frame is redirected into the handler with both addresses
as its first two arguments.
A stack the frame does not fit on ends the process instead, since a
handler is a promise the program keeps with its own stack.
.B noted
with
.B NCONT
reads the frame back and resumes it, rebuilding the parts a program must
not choose (the privilege level, the flags it does not own) from the
kernel's own constants rather than trusting what came back through
.IR copy_in .
.B NDFLT
takes the default action, which is the ending the note always was.
One delivery runs at a time.
A note posted while a handler is running
stays flagged and lands at the first boundary after
.B noted
returns.
.I Sys_notepg
fans a note out to every process in a note group but the poster, the
handler behind
.BR notepg .
.SS Wait, exit and the reaper
A deliberate
.B SYS_EXIT
or
.B SYS_EXITS
and a fault both end a process the same way from here on: the
.B Exit
record is filled in and
.B done
is set.
.I Wait_pid
is
.IR wait 's
kernel half: it collects one ended child by pid, and only a live parent
may collect its own.
A detached process (see
.B RFNOWAIT
above) has no parent to collect it, so a background reaper thread calls
.I reap_orphans
instead, which sweeps the table for every detached, ended process and
tears each one down.
.PP
When a process ends,
.I reparent_children
walks the table and hands every one of its live children to the kernel:
.B parent
zero,
.B detached
true.
That is reparent-to-init with the kernel standing in for init, and it is
what keeps a child from naming a parent pid that will never call
.I wait
again.
.SS Segments
A segment is the frames behind one contiguous mapping, reference
counted, so a frame mapped into two processes under
.B RFMEM
has one owner that outlives neither.
A process holds up to
.B MAX_PROC_SEGS
.RI ( "forty" )
of them.
.I Sys_segattach
maps the device behind an open descriptor (a framebuffer, so far) into
the caller.
That segment is shared by every fork unconditionally, since
there is one piece of hardware behind it.
.I Sys_segalloc
gives the caller a fresh run of anonymous memory, up to
.B SEGALLOC_MAX
.RI ( "four megabytes" )
at once, optionally shared across a fork under
.BR SEGSHARED .
.I Sys_segbrk
moves the top of a run the caller already holds, Plan 9's
.IR brk ,
and only the most recently allocated run may grow.
.I Sys_segdetach
unmaps a run the caller attached or allocated and, at the last release,
frees its frames.
The three segments a process was born with (text, data,
stack) may not be detached this way.
Text is always shared across a fork.
Writable data is shared only under
.BR RFMEM .
A stack is always a private copy, because writability alone cannot say
which writable segment is the stack, so the loader marks it at birth.
.SS Resolver hooks
Two synchronous devices need to know which process is asking without
importing this package directly:
.IR srv (3)
registers
.B Fd_Resolver
with
.IR resolve_fd_chan ,
which turns a descriptor number in the calling process's own table into
the chan behind it, and
.IR env (3)
registers
.B Group_Resolver
with
.IR resolve_env_group ,
which answers the calling process's environment group.
Both run under the resolving device's own spinlock, on the calling
thread, and both read
.IR current ,
the process behind the thread the scheduler is running right now.
.SS The ring 3 test programs
.B kernel/user/programs
holds a set of small programs, written in Odin with no runtime of their
own: no context, no allocator, no bounds checks, only the door.
One is picked per build and linked into a flat blob at
.BR TEXT_VA .
.B kernel/user/verify.odin
loads and runs each one to check what ring 3 can and cannot do: a program
that spins until the tick catches it, ones that fault on purpose, ones
that open files, fork, post a service, catch a note, or answer 9P by
hand.
What the kernel checks afterward is read from its own side of the
boundary the program never crosses: the mark a program left in its data
page, the trap frame's place inside the faulting thread's own kernel
stack, and a kernel global the program tried and failed to change.
